    "title": "九上阅读 · 十三 彩虹的相关科学知识",
    "passage": "Rainbows are created by light shining through water or clear objects. In nature, rainbows usually appear where rain and sunlight meet. Rainbows are beautiful jokes of light. Science, though, has unlocked how they work. Where and When Rainbows Appear\n\nIf you see a rainbow, it usually means it is raining in front of you and the sun is shining behind you. Rainbows appear in  rain or mist (雾). You need both water, in the air and direct sunlight to see one. If the sky is too cloudy, you won’t see a rainbow. The best time to see a rainbow is in the early morning or late afternoon. At that time, the sun is at a good angle (角度). Sometimes, you may even see two or three rainbows together. There are even rainbows at night made by moonlight. They are called moonbows.\n\nA rainbow looks like a half circle from the ground, but it is actually a full circle. Sometimes, airplane pilots can see the whole circle from the sky. How Rainbows Form\n\nA French scientist named René Descartes studied rainbows over 400 years ago. He used sunlight and a water-filled ball to do experiments. He learned that to see a rainbow, you must be looking at the light from the right angle. Understanding rainbows helps to know how light travels. Light moves at different speeds through different substances. For example, light moves faster in air than in water. When light enters water, it slows down and changes direction. This is why a pencil in a glass of water looks bent (弯曲的) .\n\nRainbows form when sunlight enters raindrops. The light bends inside each drop, reflects off the inside, and bends again when it comes out. This happens in millions of drops at the same time, creating all the colors of a rainbow.",
    "passageHtml": "<div style=\"text-align:center;margin:8px 0\"><img src=\"assets/images/jr_read9a_13_1.png\" alt=\"插图\" style=\"width:238px;max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:4px\"></div><p><span class=\"ind\">Rainbows are created by light shining through water or clear objects. In nature, rainbows usually appear where rain and sunlight meet. <u>Rainbows are beautiful jokes of light. </u>Science, though, has unlocked how they work. <b>Where and When Rainbows Appear</b></span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">If you see a rainbow, it usually means it is raining in front of you and the sun is shining behind you. Rainbows appear in <img src=\"assets/images/jr_read9a_13_2.png\" alt=\"插图\" style=\"height:27px;width:auto;vertical-align:middle;margin:0 4px\"> rain or mist (雾). You need both water, in the air and direct sunlight to see one. If the sky is too cloudy, you won’t see a rainbow. The best time to see a rainbow is in the early morning or late afternoon. At that time, the sun is at a good angle (角度). Sometimes, you may even see two or three rainbows together. There are even rainbows at night made by moonlight. They are called moonbows.</span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">A rainbow looks like a half circle from the ground, but it is actually a full circle. Sometimes, airplane pilots can see the whole circle from the sky. <b>How Rainbows Form</b></span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">A French scientist named René Descartes studied rainbows over 400 years ago. He used sunlight and a water-filled ball to do experiments. He learned that to see a rainbow, you must be looking at the light from the right angle. Understanding rainbows helps to know how light travels. Light moves at different speeds through different <u>substances</u>. <u>For example, light moves faster in air than in water. When light enters water, it slows down and changes direction. </u>This is why a pencil in a glass of water looks bent (弯曲的) .</span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">Rainbows form when sunlight enters raindrops. The light bends inside each drop, reflects off the inside, and bends again when it comes out. This happens in millions of drops at the same time, creating all the colors of a rainbow.</span></p>",
    "intro": "本文是一篇说明文，主要介绍了彩虹的相关科学知识，包括彩虹出现的时间、地点、形态，以及彩虹形成的科学原理。",
