    "title": "九上阅读 · 四十三 篇目四十三",
    "passage": "Dear Dr. Brown,\nI feel stressed recently because I am always busy with my study. I think there isn’t enough time to do so many things. But most of my classmates can finish their homework early and spare some time to play with friends. I don’t know why.\nLi Hua\nDear Li Hua,\nThank you for your question. Of course, I would be glad to help you. From your letter, I learn that you may not know how to plan your time wisely. Don’t be nervous! Here is my advice.\nGet a daily planner. Use a piece of paper for scheduling (行程安排). Map out exactly how you will use your time each day, including how much time you would like to spend on each task. For example, start your schedule with something like “8:00 a.m.-8:30 a.m.: Review English notes”.\n________ It’s hard to get things done when you’re doing too many things at once. As you cross things off your to-do list, let your mind focus on the task at hand. It’s much more efficient to deal with one responsibility at a time.\nReview your task list at the end of each day. Cross off the tasks you’ve finished and make a new to-do list for the next day. This can help you celebrate what you’ve achieved while keeping your goals for the next day in mind. In this way, you can make full preparations for them.\nWell, I hope the above has answered your question, and I sincerely hope my advice can help you.\nDr. Brown",
    "passageHtml": "<div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:10px 0\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid var(--bd);font-size:14px\"><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid var(--bd);padding:8px 12px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;line-height:1.65\"><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Dear Dr. Brown,</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">I feel stressed recently because I am always busy with my study. I think there isn’t enough time to do so many things. But most of my classmates can finish their homework early and spare some time to play with friends. I don’t know why.</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Li Hua</p></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid var(--bd);padding:8px 12px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;line-height:1.65\"><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Dear Li Hua,</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Thank you for your question. Of course, I would be glad to help you. From your letter, I learn that you may not know how to plan your time wisely. Don’t be nervous! Here is my advice.</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Get a daily planner. Use a piece of paper for scheduling (行程安排). Map out exactly how you will use your time each day, including how much time you would like to spend on each task. For example, start your schedule with something like “8:00 a.m.-8:30 a.m.: Review English notes”.</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">________ It’s hard to get things done when you’re doing too many things at once. As you cross things off your to-do list, let your mind focus on the task at hand. It’s much more efficient to deal with one responsibility at a time.</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Review your task list at the end of each day. Cross off the tasks you’ve finished and make a new to-do list for the next day. This can help you celebrate what you’ve achieved while keeping your goals for the next day in mind. In this way, you can make full preparations for them.</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Well, I hope the above has answered your question, and I sincerely hope my advice can help you.</p><p style=\"margin:4px 0;text-align:left\">Dr. Brown</p></td></tr></table></div>",
